[38;5;252m[1m## What I found[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mOne real translation bug: exit status on a broken stdout pipe.[22m The Rust runtime sets [38;5;10mSIGPIPE[0m to [38;5;10mSIG_IGN[0m before [38;5;10mmain[0m; the C program keeps the default. Writing to a pipe with no reader kills the C program with signal 13, while Rust discarded the [38;5;10mEPIPE[0m (exactly as C discards [38;5;10mprintf[0m's return value) and exited 0. Fixed by resetting [38;5;10mSIGPIPE[0m to [38;5;10mSIG_DFL[0m at the top of [38;5;10mmain[0m, using a direct [38;5;10msignal(2)[0m declaration since the crate has no dependencies.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mOne test-harness bug that was hiding it.[22m My first broken-pipe test used [38;5;10mpipe(2)[0m, whose fds lack close-on-exec, so the child inherited the read end — the pipe still had a reader, the write succeeded, and [3mboth[23m programs exited 0. The test passed while measuring nothing. Switching to [38;5;10mpipe2(fds, O_CLOEXEC)[0m made it discriminate. I only caught this because I mutation-tested the suite: deleting the [38;5;10mSIGPIPE[0m fix didn't make anything fail.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The scanf and arithmetic translation was already correct, including the parts that look wrong: [38;5;10m%d[0m skipping newlines, conversion failure silently leaving [38;5;10mx[0m at 0 and still exiting 0, [38;5;10m\0[0m counting as a matching failure, [38;5;10m2*x+300[0m wrapping in 32-bit [38;5;10mint[0m, and [38;5;10mstrtol[0m saturating at [38;5;10mLONG_MAX[0m before truncation to [38;5;10mint[0m — which is why every huge positive input prints [38;5;10m298[0m and every huge negative one prints [38;5;10m300[0m.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
